I have wanted to become a professor for a long time because, to me, one of the most rewarding parts of academia is watching people grow into researchers and mentors in their own right. I am still learning what it means to be a good advisor, and I expect that I will continue learning throughout my career. In many ways, that is also one of the things I find most exciting about academia: there is always more to learn about how to support, encourage, and mentor people well.

I have been fortunate to have excellent advisors and mentors throughout my own journey, each of whom has shaped how I think about research, mentorship, and academic life. I hope to carry forward the lessons I have learned from them while developing an advising style of my own. This statement reflects how I currently think about mentorship, what I hope to provide as an advisor, and the kind of research community I hope to build in PEARL.

## Goal of a Ph.D.

The goal of a Ph.D. is to help shape the next generation of researchers. I want students to leave PEARL as independent researchers who can identify important problems, design careful studies or systems, and communicate their work clearly.

A central part of this is learning how to advance knowledge: identifying interesting and impactful research questions, distilling them into actionable projects, and developing the technical expertise needed to answer them. Just as important is learning how to communicate what you have found. To me, research is not only about making discoveries, but also about clearly conveying why they matter and inspiring others to build on them. I therefore encourage students to develop strong writing and presentation skills and to actively participate in the broader research community through conferences and workshops. 

A Ph.D. is also a time to learn how to support and mentor other researchers. By the time you graduate, my hope is that you feel confident not only as an independent researcher, but also as a collaborator, community member, and mentor to others.

## What I Look for in a Graduate Student

* **Personal attributes:** [Grit](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grit_%28personality_trait%29) is valuable in graduate school because research is difficult by nature, and rejected papers and failed ideas are practically inevitable. Another very important trait I look for is being comfortable working independently while also being a good collaborator. 

* **Initiative and proactivity:** I look for people with high agency—students who understand that they are in the driver's seat of their Ph.D., not me as their advisor or anyone else, and who have internalized that and work accordingly. I want students who take on new initiatives, propose ambitious research directions, seek out collaborators who can help make that work possible, and are gritty enough to see it through.

* **Strong writing skills:** Writing is a major part of research. I may work with students who are still developing as writers, but strong writing skills make it much easier to communicate ideas, publish research, and eventually write a dissertation. When I read applicants' writing samples, I pay attention to everything from the overall organization and coherence of the argument to sentence-level clarity and word choice. 

* **Critical thinking:** Strong researchers need to question assumptions, identify weaknesses in an argument or approach, and reason carefully about what evidence actually supports. This can be difficult to assess directly, so I look for signs of it in writing samples, prior research, and conversations with potential advisees.

* **Attention to detail:** Careful research depends on details, whether in experimental design, implementation, analysis, or writing. When students are attentive to these details, I can spend more of our time together helping them sharpen the bigger research questions and produce their best work.

* **Prior research experience:** Experience working in a research lab, whether through an REU, a longer-term collaboration, or another research program, is valuable. Having a publication at a major venue is a plus, but it is not a requirement. I generally place less weight on class projects unless they develop substantially beyond the course itself. Similarly, independent projects are most informative to me when they demonstrate sustained research effort and lead to a concrete research outcome.

## My Role as Your Advisor

My job as your advisor is to help you identify the right problems to work on, develop the tools needed to solve them, and frame your research in a way that communicates its contribution and impact clearly. Ideas in research are plentiful, but not every idea is worth pursuing. I will help you recognize which questions have the greatest potential for impact, turn them into tractable technical problems, and determine how to answer them convincingly. I generally prefer not to chase low-hanging fruit simply because it is available. Instead, I encourage students to think about their strengths, interests, and what they may be uniquely positioned to contribute. For every paper, I like to identify the core insight and build the work around it. My goal is for us to produce research that people remember and that influences how they think, even after the particular model, dataset, or task we studied has become outdated.

I also see it as my responsibility to help you become visible within the broader research community by supporting conference travel, introducing you to collaborators, and creating opportunities for you to engage with researchers beyond our group. Conferences have been one of my favorite parts of my own research career, and I hope my students have the opportunity to enjoy that aspect of academic life as well. More broadly, I see myself as an advocate for your success during the Ph.D. and beyond.

## My Advising Style

I expect to meet with students one-on-one for at least half an hour each week, although the exact structure will be flexible. Around deadlines, we may meet more often. Travel or other commitments may occasionally require us to skip a week. Students working closely together on larger projects may also find that group meetings are more useful than individual ones at certain stages. These meetings can cover anything from high-level project framing and technical problems to paper writing, career planning, or whatever else is most useful at the time. We will also have regular group meetings where we discuss each other's research, share career and professional-development advice, and sometimes simply spend time together as a group playing board games! I also plan to hold weekly research office hours for anyone who has a question or obstacle that is preventing them from making progress.

My advising style will depend on the student, their working style, and where they are in their Ph.D. I try to balance structure with autonomy. Early on, I expect us to define concrete milestones and check in relatively frequently. As you gain experience and confidence, I expect you to take increasing ownership of the research direction, while I remain available as a sounding board, collaborator, co-author, and advocate.

PEARL is still in its early stages, but I envision it growing to roughly 8–10 Ph.D. students alongside a rotating group of undergraduate students, Master's students, and visitors. I strongly believe that research is a team sport, and I want to build a group where people actively help one another. I will encourage collaborations within the lab and give Ph.D. students opportunities to mentor junior researchers. Some of my own strongest research experiences have come from working in highly collaborative teams, and I hope to create an environment where those kinds of collaborations happen naturally.

I am also a new advisor, which means I will be learning and refining how I mentor students. I want students to tell me what is working and what is not. Open and honest communication is important to me. If something about our working relationship is not helping you, I would much rather know so that we can figure out how to improve it together.

## Work–Life Balance

A Ph.D. is a long journey, and it can be difficult. There is no point in sugarcoating that. At the same time, maintaining a life outside of research is essential for a healthy, happy, and sustainable career. Having time to disconnect, pursue hobbies, taking time to care for your physical and mental health, spend time with people you care about, and do things completely unrelated to research can make you both happier and more creative. Sometimes, less really is more. If you have been stuck on a problem for hours, stepping away and returning with a fresh perspective can be far more productive than continuing to push through it. You should not stop living your life simply because you are doing a Ph.D.

I also want PEARL to be a community rather than simply a collection of people working in the same place. I hope we will regularly do things together outside of research, whether that means dinners, picnics, board games, or other activities that people in the group enjoy. I would love for students to share their own interests and hobbies with one another as well. I have plenty of hobbies of my own to contribute, and I fully expect to pick up a few new ones from the people who join the group.
